LVM - Logical Volume Management.

sudo pacman -S lvm2
hooks required - lvm2

**Keep some extra space so that you can have snapshots, extend volgroups in case if its required etc**

disk -> 
    format as a physical volume that lvm can use -> 
        Use one or more PVs to create a volume group -> 
            Create one/more logical volumes on top.

pvdisplay - display information about the physical volume.
pvcreate disk_indentifier - designate a disk to be used as a physical volume.

vgdisplay - display information about the volume group.
vgcreate volgroupname pv - Create a volgroup in the specific physical volume pv.
vgextend volgroup pv - extend volgroup to use physical volume pv.

lvs - display some information about the logical volumes.
lvdisplay - display information about the logical volumes present in a volgroup.
lvcreate volgroupname -L +5G -n "lv_name"; -l +100%FREE to use all the space available in the volgroup.
lvextend -L +10G /dev/mapper/volgroupname-lvname - Extend lv lvname by 10G.
resize2fs /dev/mapper/volgroupname-lvname - resize the fs so that it is shown in (df -h).

lvextend --resizefs .. = Perform resize2fs after extending the logical volume.
lvremove /dev/volgroup0/lv_foo

/dev/volgroupname/lv_name - disk path.

/dev/mapper/volgroupname-lv_name - can be used to create fs, automount using /etc/fstab etc.

Snapshots
/dev/mapper/volgroup0-lv_webpage
lvcreate /dev/mapper/volgroup0-lv_webpage -L +1G -s -n webpage_snapshot => now webpage_snapshot has origin lv_webpage

Recovering the changes.
    1. Unmount the target (lv_webpage)
    2. lvconvert --merge /dev/mapper/volgroup0-webpage_snapshot (Overwrite the origin lv).
    3. lvchange -an /dev/mapper/volgroup0-lv_webpage (deactivate)
    4. lvchange -ay /dev/mapper/volgroup0-lv_webpage (reactivate)
    5. Mount and use lv_webpage

Snapshots are temporary and are not recommended for backups.
Best use cases - test changes and revert if not required.
